    bio: 'Mary Brenda Hesse was born in Totnes, Devon, England, and educated at Imperial College London, where she studied mathematics and physics before turning to the philosophy of science. She spent the majority of her academic career at Cambridge University, where she was a fellow of Wolfson College and eventually became Professor of Philosophy of Science — one of the first women to hold a professorial chair in that faculty. Her intellectual formation brought together a deep knowledge of the physical sciences, a rigorous training in formal logic, and a historical sensibility shaped by her engagement with the work of Pierre Duhem and the Cambridge tradition of philosophy of science represented by figures like R.B. Braithwaite.\n\nHesse\'s most celebrated contribution to the philosophy of science was her theory of scientific models and analogical reasoning, developed in her landmark 1966 book Models and Analogies in Science. Against the received view that scientific explanation consists in the logical derivation of observational statements from theoretical laws, Hesse argued that the engine of scientific discovery and understanding is the use of models — structural analogies between a familiar domain and an unfamiliar one. When scientists explain electrical current by analogy with water flowing through pipes, or atomic structure by analogy with a miniature solar system, they are not producing mere pedagogical aids. They are doing the constitutive cognitive work of science: using the structure of the familiar to extend understanding into the unknown. Hesse developed a formal account of how positive, negative, and neutral analogies interact in the construction and testing of scientific models.\n\nIn her later work, including her 1974 book The Structure of Scientific Inference, Hesse turned to questions about the underdetermination of theory by evidence and the role of coherence and simplicity in scientific reasoning, anticipating themes that would later become central to debates about scientific realism. She also engaged seriously with the sociology of scientific knowledge, taking seriously the claim that social and cultural factors shape scientific theory without retreating to a simple relativism. Hesse remained intellectually active into old age, writing on artificial intelligence, the philosophy of mind, and the relationship between science and theology.',
